A||N||6||Seanh||Where in the heck (or in the engine) does the stupid dipstick go?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||03-29-2001||01:40 AM||||Well, I couldn't for a million dollars find the hole in the block for the dipstick tube to go in, where is it? I dont see it anywhere. Also, does it bolt where the bracket on the front of the engine bolts to the coolant crossover pipe? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Hank is Here||03-29-2001||02:08 AM||||Which engine the 2.5 or 2.8. On both engines they are aginst the firewall, that is what happens with FWD engines in RWD cars<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fyerstarter||03-29-2001||02:13 AM||||hehehe. Oh it's a *****  allright. My dipstick tube had to be relocated a bit in order to accomodate the headers and apparently whoever put them on didn't do a good job of re-attaching the bracket. I went to check the oil and the whole tube came out in my hand!<P>If I recall correctly on the V-6 the hole is somewhat in the same vicinity as the oil filter. You gotta jack the car up and feel around from underneath between the motor and the firewall. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||03-29-2001||05:41 AM||||on the 2.8 the dipstick hole is in the lower edge of the block to the right of the oil filter and slightly to the left of the left end of the starter..........that is, if you are facing the rear of the car.......||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||03-29-2001||05:42 AM||||oh yeah.........there is a rubber o-ring on the bottom of the dipstick tube (normally) when you put the tube into the hole you need to push it in place until you feel it snap in.......||||reg||1
Z||000005||operator64||03-29-2001||06:45 AM||||   I have a special Fiero..... The dipstick sits in the drivers seat! LOL<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||03-29-2001||12:26 PM||||Save your money.  Being the generous guy I am, I'll fly out to Nebraska, and show you where the dipstick goes, for only $100,000.99!    Such a deal!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>To install the dipstick, I jacked the car up, stuck the tube down into roughly its installed location.  Then crawled under the car and positioned the lower end of the tube near the hole in the block - it goes into a hole in the top of a little bump that is "sculpted" into the lower part of the forward face of the block.  Didn't have enough strength to force the tube into the hole.<P>Asked my neighbor to help me at this point.  As I guided the tube into the hole from below, he gradually applied force from above.  <BR>Worked great!<P>I think you are right - the little screw that fastens the tube, threads into the coolant crossover bracket, if I remember correctly.  It's a little 7mm-head hex screw, I think.<P>**Second thought - Now I think the tube-attach screw threads into a hole in one of the brackets closer to the top of the engine - on the forward side of the forward head.<P>It's just a little threaded hole, in a black enamel steel bracket - not a terribly obvious one.  Almost looks like an afterthought.  But when you get the dipstick rotated into place, its (welded-on) bracket will guide you to it.  <P>Sorry about my memory - or lack of   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">            <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 03-29-2001).]||||reg||12
